1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,890
In public discussion of business, we take certain values for granted.

2
00:00:06,890 --> 00:00:10,340
Today I'm going to talk about four of them

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:16,310
collaboration, hard work, creativity and excellence.

4
00:00:16,310 --> 00:00:20,120
Most people would say they're all 'good things

5
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:23,970
I'm going to suggest that's an over-simple view.

6
00:00:23,970 --> 00:00:28,805
The trouble with these values is that they're theoretical concepts,

7
00:00:28,805 --> 00:00:32,190
removed from the reality of day-to-day business.

8
00:00:32,190 --> 00:00:38,610
Pursue values by all means, but be prepared for what may happen as a result.

9
00:00:38,610 --> 00:00:43,560
They can actually cause damage, which is not at all the intention.

10
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:47,750
Business leaders generally try to do the right thing.

11
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:51,143
But all too often the right thing backfires,

12
00:00:51,143 --> 00:00:54,620
if those leaders adopt values without understanding

13
00:00:54,620 --> 00:00:57,081
and managing the side effects that arise.

14
00:00:57,081 --> 00:01:02,170
The values can easily get in the way of what's actually intended.

15
00:01:02,170 --> 00:01:08,480
OK. So the first value I'm going to discuss is collaboration.

16
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:11,208
Er, let me give you an example.

17
00:01:11,208 --> 00:01:16,274
On a management training course I once attended, we were put into groups

18
00:01:16,274 --> 00:01:19,500
and had to construct a bridge across a stream

19
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:22,510
using building blocks that we were given.

20
00:01:22,510 --> 00:01:30,170
The rule was that everyone in the team had to move at least one building block during the construction.

21
00:01:30,170 --> 00:01:33,160
This was intended to encourage teamwork

22
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:37,970
But it was really a job best done by one person.

23
00:01:37,970 --> 00:01:42,075
The other teams tried to collaborate on building the structure,

24
00:01:42,075 --> 00:01:46,590
and descended into confusion, with everyone getting in each other's way.

25
00:01:46,590 --> 00:01:50,410
Our team leader solved the challenge brilliantly.

26
00:01:50,410 --> 00:01:55,040
She simply asked everyone in the team to move a piece a few centimetres

27
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:56,767
to comply with the rule,

28
00:01:56,767 --> 00:02:02,790
and then let the person in the team with an aptitude for puzzles like this build it alone.

29
00:02:02,790 --> 00:02:05,703
We finished before any other team.

30
00:02:05,703 --> 00:02:13,470
My point is that the task wasn't really suited to teamworking, so why make it one?

31
00:02:13,470 --> 00:02:20,320
Teamwork can also lead to inconsistency - a common cause of poor sales.

32
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:25,083
In the case of a smartphone that a certain company launched,

33
00:02:25,083 --> 00:02:28,200
one director wanted to target the business market

34
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:32,010
and another demanded it was aimed at consumers.

35
00:02:32,010 --> 00:02:36,050
The company wanted both directors to be involved,

36
00:02:36,050 --> 00:02:42,110
so gave the product a consumer-friendly name, but marketed it to companies.

37
00:02:42,110 --> 00:02:46,300
The result was that it met the needs of neither group.

38
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:55,619
It would have been better to let one director or the other have his way, not both.

39
00:02:55,619 --> 00:02:58,360
Now industriousness, or hard work.

40
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:02,556
It's easy to mock people who say they work hard:

41
00:03:02,556 --> 00:03:08,850
after all, a hamster running around in a wheel is working hard - and getting nowhere.

42
00:03:08,850 --> 00:03:14,300
Of course hard work is valuable, but only when properly targeted.

43
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:20,053
Otherwise it wastes the resources that companies value most - time and energy.

44
00:03:20,053 --> 00:03:22,930
And that's bad for the organisation.

45
00:03:22,930 --> 00:03:29,874
There's a management model that groups people according to four criteria:

46
00:03:29,874 --> 00:03:33,030
clever, hard-working, stupid and lazy.

47
00:03:33,030 --> 00:03:39,375
Here 'lazy' means having a rational determination not to carry out unnecessary tasks.

48
00:03:39,375 --> 00:03:43,280
It doesn't mean trying to avoid work altogether.

49
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:47,000
Most people display two of these characteristics

50
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,248
and the most valuable people are those

51
00:03:50,248 --> 00:03:58,710
who are both clever and lazy: they possess intellectual clarity, and they don't rush into making decisions.

52
00:03:58,710 --> 00:04:01,960
They come up with solutions to save the time

53
00:04:01,960 --> 00:04:05,730
and energy spent by the stupid and hard-working group.

54
00:04:05,730 --> 00:04:08,800
Instead of throwing more man-hours at a problem

55
00:04:09,180 --> 00:04:13,700
the clever and lazy group looks for a more effective solution.

56
00:04:13,700 --> 00:04:19,186
Next we come to creativity. This often works well -

57
00:04:19,186 --> 00:04:23,660
creating an attention-grabbing TV commercial, for example

58
00:04:23,940 --> 00:04:26,500
might lead to increased sales.

59
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:30,053
But it isn't always a good thing.

60
00:04:30,053 --> 00:04:34,120
Some advertising campaigns are remembered for their creativity

61
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:37,160
without having any effect on sales.

62
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:42,234
This happened a few years ago with the launch of a chocolate bar:

63
00:04:42,234 --> 00:04:46,137
subsequent research showed that plenty of consumers remembered the adverts,

64
00:04:46,137 --> 00:04:49,260
but had no idea what was being advertised.

65
00:04:49,260 --> 00:04:54,720
The trouble is that the creator derives pleasure from coming up with the idea,

66
00:04:54,720 --> 00:04:59,400
and wrongly assumes the audience for the campaign will share that feeling.

67
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:08,213
A company that brings out thousands of new products may seem more creative than a company that only has a few,

68
00:05:08,213 --> 00:05:12,410
but it may be too creative, and make smaller profits.

69
00:05:12,410 --> 00:05:19,120
Creativity needs to be targeted, to solve a problem that the company has identified.

70
00:05:19,120 --> 00:05:21,060
Just coming up with more

71
00:05:21,060 --> 00:05:27,990
and more novel products isn't necessarily a good thing. And finally, excellence.

72
00:05:27,990 --> 00:05:32,180
We all know companies that claim they 'strive for excellence

73
00:05:32,280 --> 00:05:36,180
but it takes a long time to achieve excellence.

74
00:05:36,180 --> 00:05:43,140
In business, being first with a product is more profitable than having the best product.

75
00:05:43,140 --> 00:05:47,096
A major study of company performance compared pioneers -

76
00:05:47,096 --> 00:05:53,030
that is, companies bringing out the first version of a particular product -

77
00:05:53,030 --> 00:05:58,470
with followers, the companies that copied and improved on that product.

78
00:05:58,470 --> 00:06:05,097
The study found that the pioneers commanded an average market share of 29 percent,

79
00:06:05,097 --> 00:06:08,883
while the followers achieved less than half that,

80
00:06:08,883 --> 00:06:14,090
only 13 percent - even though their product might have been better.

81
00:06:14,090 --> 00:06:19,135
Insisting on excellence in everything we do is time-consuming,

82
00:06:19,135 --> 00:06:24,180
wastes energy and leads to losing out on opportunities.

83
00:06:24,180 --> 00:06:29,700
Sometimes, second-rate work is more worthwhile than excellence.

84
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:35,184
'Make sure it's excellent' sounds like a good approach to business,

85
00:06:35,184 --> 00:06:40,170
but the 'just-get-started' approach is likely to be more successful.
